|---|-------|-------------|
| 0 | 0     | Base case   |
| 1 | -1    | Base case   |
| 2 | -1    | Base case   |
| 3 | -2    | 0 + (-1) + (-1) |
| 4 | -4    | (-1) + (-1) + (-2) |
| 5 | -7    | (-1) + (-2) + (-4) |
| 6 | -13   | (-2) + (-4) + (-7) |

## Note on Performance

Like the Fibonacci recursion, this approach has a time complexity of **O(3ⁿ)** due to repeated subproblem calculations. For larger values of `n`, consider:

1. **Memoization** – Store previously computed results in a map/array.
2. **Iterative approach** – Use a loop with three variables to track the last three values.

### Iterative Version (More Efficient)

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int triboonacciIterative(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    if (n == 1) return -1;
    if (n == 2) return -1;

    int a = 0, b = -1, c = -1;
    for (int i = 3; i <= n; i++) {
        int next = a + b + c;
        a = b;
        b = c;
        c = next;
    }
    return c;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    std::cout << "Enter the value of n: ";
    std::cin >> n;

    if (n < 0) {
        std::cout << "Please enter a non-negative integer." << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    std::cout << "The " << n << "th number in the sequence is: " << triboonacciIterative(n) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

The iterative version runs in **O(n)** time and **O(1)** space, making it suitable for much larger values of `n`.

